<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.10.1: The Vertex and Intercepts of a Function</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02498</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.10.1: The Vertex and Intercepts of a Function</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1f73e2ff-19eb-46cf-b6bd-af66cf5b195a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>These expressions each define the same function.</para>
<list>
  <item> \(x^2+6x+8\) </item>
  <item> \((x+2)(x+4)\) </item>
  <item> \((x+3)^2−1\) </item>
</list>
<para>Without graphing or doing any calculations, determine where the following features would be on a graph that represents the function.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Warm Up Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="6f6efa7e-aa7e-4569-8a0f-891502cd7b1b"><problem><para>
      <para>The \(x\)-coordinate of the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">vertex (of a graph)</term>.</para>
      <para>Enter the \(x\)-coordinate.</para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>-3</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is -3. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is -3.</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="380ada69-6116-46ec-b904-b6ac6d41ea2f"><problem><para>
      <para><emphasis>Select both</emphasis> <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">\(x\)-intercepts</term>.</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>["\\((-2,0)\\)", "\\((-4,0)\\)"]</para><para>
      <para>The correct answers are: \((-2,0)\), \((-4,0)\). The \(x\)-intercepts are \((-2,0)\) and \((-4,0)\).</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="8dd03ced-8b05-43a1-b1e7-bf429fcbcb0b"><problem><para>
      <para>The \(y\)-coordinate of the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">\(y\)-intercept</term> \((0, \_\_\_\_\_)\)</para>
      <para>Enter the \(y\)-coordinate of the \(y\)-intercept. </para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>8</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is 8. The \(y\)-intercept is \((0,8)\).</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
</document>
